 "questions": [
  {
   "xml_index": 1,
   "xml_name": "L07_001_Create A List",
   "display_name": "Create A List",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Welcome to Lab 7! This lab is all about <b>lists</b>, one of the most useful things in Python.</p>\n<p>A <b>list</b> is an ordered sequence of values, called <b>items</b> (or elements). You write a list with square brackets <code>[ ]</code>, and separate the items with commas:</p>\n<pre>colours = [&quot;red&quot;, &quot;green&quot;, &quot;blue&quot;, &quot;yellow&quot;, &quot;black&quot;]</pre>\n<p>Each item has a position, called its <b>index</b>. Counting starts at <b>0</b>:</p>\n<table style=\"border-collapse:separate;border-spacing:2px 0;margin:6px 0 4px\"><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace\">colours =</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(31,95,153);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">&#x27;red&#x27;</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(42,112,170);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">&#x27;green&#x27;</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(52,129,188);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">&#x27;blue&#x27;</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(63,146,206);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">&#x27;yellow&#x27;</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(74,163,223);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">&#x27;black&#x27;</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px\"></td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">0</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">1</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">2</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">3</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">4</td></tr></table>\n<p>A list can hold any kind of value: numbers, strings, <code>True</code>/<code>False</code>, even a mix of these, and even other lists. <code>len(L)</code> gives the number of items. Printing a list shows it with its brackets:</p>\n<pre>marks = [72, 85, 90]\nmixed = [1, &quot;abc&quot;, 2.5, True]\nprint(marks)\nprint(len(marks))\nprint(mixed)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>[72, 85, 90]\n3\n[1, &#x27;abc&#x27;, 2.5, True]</pre>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>make_list(a, b, c)</code>, which returns a new list containing <code>a</code>, <code>b</code> and <code>c</code>, in that order. The three values can be of any type.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>make_list(1, 2, 3)</code> returns <code>[1, 2, 3]</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def make_list(a, b, c):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(make_list(1, 2, 3))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
