  {
   "xml_index": 2,
   "xml_name": "L07_002_Build A List With Append",
   "display_name": "Build A List With Append",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Often we do not know the items in advance, and build the list <b>step by step</b>:</p>\n<ol><li>Start with an <b>empty list</b> <code>[]</code> (it has 0 items).</li><li>Add items one at a time with <code>L.append(x)</code>, which puts <code>x</code> at the <b>end</b> of the list.</li></ol>\n<p>You met <code>append</code> briefly in Lab 5. Here it is again, step by step:</p>\n<pre>squares = []\nprint(squares, len(squares))\nfor k in range(1, 5):\n    squares.append(k * k)\n    print(squares)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>[] 0\n[1]\n[1, 4]\n[1, 4, 9]\n[1, 4, 9, 16]</pre>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, loop or function must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>numbers_up_to(n)</code>, which returns the list <code>[1, 2, ..., n]</code> for a non-negative integer <code>n</code> (int &gt;= 0). For <code>n = 0</code> it returns the empty list <code>[]</code>. Build it with an empty list and <code>append</code>.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>numbers_up_to(5)</code> returns <code>[1, 2, 3, 4, 5]</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def numbers_up_to(n):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(numbers_up_to(5))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(numbers_up_to(5))",
