  {
   "xml_index": 4,
   "xml_name": "L07_004_List From A Range",
   "display_name": "List From A Range",
   "questiontext_html": "<p><code>range(start, stop, step)</code> produces numbers, but it is not a list itself. <code>list(range(...))</code> converts it into one, which is handy to <b>see</b> exactly which numbers a range produces. Remember: the range stops <b>before</b> <code>stop</code>.</p>\n<pre>print(list(range(5)))\nprint(list(range(2, 12, 3)))\nprint(list(range(10, 0, -2)))\nprint(list(range(4, 4)))</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>[0, 1, 2, 3, 4]\n[2, 5, 8, 11]\n[10, 8, 6, 4, 2]\n[]</pre>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>range_list(start, stop, step)</code>, which returns the list of numbers produced by <code>range(start, stop, step)</code>. The integers are such that <code>step</code> is never 0.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>range_list(1, 6, 1)</code> returns <code>[1, 2, 3, 4, 5]</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def range_list(start, stop, step):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(range_list(1, 6, 1))\n",
