  {
   "xml_index": 5,
   "xml_name": "L07_005_Numbers From A Line Of Text",
   "display_name": "Numbers From A Line Of Text",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Input often arrives as one line of text, like <code>\"3 14 15\"</code>. The string method <code>text.split()</code> cuts a string at the spaces and returns a <b>list of strings</b> (the words):</p>\n<pre>line = &quot;the quick  brown fox&quot;\nwords = line.split()\nprint(words)\nprint(len(words))\nprint(&quot;12 7 30&quot;.split())</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>[&#x27;the&#x27;, &#x27;quick&#x27;, &#x27;brown&#x27;, &#x27;fox&#x27;]\n4\n[&#x27;12&#x27;, &#x27;7&#x27;, &#x27;30&#x27;]</pre>\n<p>Notice that <code>\"12 7 30\".split()</code> gives the <b>strings</b> <code>'12'</code>, <code>'7'</code> and <code>'30'</code>, not numbers. To get numbers, convert each one with <code>int(...)</code> and append it to a new list.</p>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, loop or function must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>to_numbers(text)</code>. The string <code>text</code> contains one or more integers separated by spaces; return them as a list of <code>int</code> values, in the same order.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>to_numbers(\"3 14 15\")</code> returns <code>[3, 14, 15]</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def to_numbers(text):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(to_numbers(\"3 14 15\"))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
