  {
   "xml_index": 6,
   "xml_name": "L07_006_First And Last Item",
   "display_name": "First And Last Item",
   "questiontext_html": "<p><code>L[i]</code> gives the item at index <code>i</code>. Besides the usual indexes <code>0, 1, 2, ...</code> counted from the <b>front</b>, Python also has <b>negative indexes</b>, counted from the <b>back</b>: <code>L[-1]</code> is the last item, <code>L[-2]</code> the one before it, and so on.</p>\n<table style=\"border-collapse:separate;border-spacing:2px 0;margin:6px 0 4px\"><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px\"></td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">-5</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">-4</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">-3</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">-2</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">-1</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace\">colours =</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(31,95,153);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">&#x27;red&#x27;</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(42,112,170);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">&#x27;green&#x27;</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(52,129,188);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">&#x27;blue&#x27;</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(63,146,206);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">&#x27;yellow&#x27;</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(74,163,223);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">&#x27;black&#x27;</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px\"></td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">0</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">1</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">2</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">3</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">4</td></tr></table>\n<p>The top row shows the negative indexes, the bottom row the positive ones. So <code>colours[4]</code> and <code>colours[-1]</code> are both <code>'black'</code>, and <code>colours[0]</code> and <code>colours[-5]</code> are both <code>'red'</code>.</p>\n<pre>colours = [&quot;red&quot;, &quot;green&quot;, &quot;blue&quot;, &quot;yellow&quot;, &quot;black&quot;]\nprint(colours[0], colours[-1])\nprint(colours[1], colours[-2])</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>red black\ngreen yellow</pre>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>first_and_last(L)</code>, which returns a new list with two items: the first item of <code>L</code> and the last item of <code>L</code>. <code>L</code> is a non-empty list (for a list with one item, that item is both first and last).</p>\n<p>For example, <code>first_and_last([\"red\", \"green\", \"blue\"])</code> returns <code>['red', 'blue']</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def first_and_last(L):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(first_and_last([\"red\", \"green\", \"blue\"]))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(first_and_last([\"red\", \"green\", \"blue\"]))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "['red', 'blue']",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(first_and_last([7]))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[7, 7]",
     "extra": "",
