  {
   "xml_index": 7,
   "xml_name": "L07_007_Kth Item From The End",
   "display_name": "Kth Item From The End",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Negative indexes make \"the k-th item from the end\" easy: it is simply <code>L[-k]</code>. Look at the diagram: counting from the back, the 1st item is at <code>-1</code>, the 2nd at <code>-2</code>, and so on.</p>\n<table style=\"border-collapse:separate;border-spacing:2px 0;margin:6px 0 4px\"><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px\"></td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">-6</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">-5</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">-4</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">-3</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">-2</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">-1</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace\">L =</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:#e2e6ea;color:#8a939c;border-radius:2px\">10</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:#e2e6ea;color:#8a939c;border-radius:2px\">20</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:#e2e6ea;color:#8a939c;border-radius:2px\">30</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(57,136,195);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">40</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:#e2e6ea;color:#8a939c;border-radius:2px\">50</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:#e2e6ea;color:#8a939c;border-radius:2px\">60</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px\"></td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">0</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">1</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">2</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">3</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">4</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">5</td></tr></table><div style=\"font-size:13px;color:#8a939c;margin:0 0 10px\">L[-3] is 40: the 3rd item from the end (the same item as L[3])</div>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>from_end(L, k)</code>, which returns the <code>k</code>-th item from the end of the list <code>L</code>, where <code>k</code> is an integer from 1 to <code>len(L)</code>.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>from_end([10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60], 3)</code> returns <code>40</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def from_end(L, k):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(from_end([10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60], 3))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
