  {
   "xml_index": 9,
   "xml_name": "L07_009_Negative Index Twin",
   "display_name": "Negative Index Twin",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Every negative index has a positive \"twin\" that points to the same item. In a list of length <code>n</code>, the negative index <code>i</code> is the same position as <code>n + i</code>:</p>\n<table style=\"border-collapse:separate;border-spacing:2px 0;margin:6px 0 4px\"><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px\"></td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">-5</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">-4</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">-3</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">-2</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">-1</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace\">colours =</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:#e2e6ea;color:#8a939c;border-radius:2px\">&#x27;red&#x27;</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(42,112,170);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">&#x27;green&#x27;</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:#e2e6ea;color:#8a939c;border-radius:2px\">&#x27;blue&#x27;</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:#e2e6ea;color:#8a939c;border-radius:2px\">&#x27;yellow&#x27;</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:#e2e6ea;color:#8a939c;border-radius:2px\">&#x27;black&#x27;</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px\"></td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">0</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">1</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">2</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">3</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">4</td></tr></table><div style=\"font-size:13px;color:#8a939c;margin:0 0 10px\">n = 5: the index -4 is the same position as 5 + (-4) = 1</div>\n<p>This is how Python itself understands negative indexes: <code>L[-1]</code> means <code>L[len(L) - 1]</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>positive_index(n, i)</code>, which returns the positive index of the same position as the negative index <code>i</code>, in a list of length <code>n</code>. Here <code>n</code> is a positive integer (int &gt; 0) and <code>i</code> is a negative integer from <code>-n</code> to <code>-1</code>.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>positive_index(5, -4)</code> returns <code>1</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def positive_index(n, i):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(positive_index(5, -4))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(positive_index(5, -4))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "1",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(positive_index(5, -1))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "4",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(positive_index(5, -5))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "0",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(positive_index(1, -1))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "0",
     "extra": "",
