  {
   "xml_index": 10,
   "xml_name": "L07_010_Safe Index",
   "display_name": "Safe Index",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>In Lab 6 you saw that an index outside the list gives an <code>IndexError</code>. For a list of length <code>n</code>, the valid indexes are <code>0</code> to <code>n - 1</code> and <code>-n</code> to <code>-1</code>. Anything else is out of range. An empty list has no valid index at all.</p>\n<p>Sometimes, instead of crashing, a function should return a special value. Python has the value <code>None</code> (meaning \"nothing\") for this.</p>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, loop or function must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>get_item(L, i)</code>, which returns <code>L[i]</code> if <code>i</code> is a valid index for the list <code>L</code> (positive or negative), and <code>None</code> otherwise. <code>i</code> can be any integer.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>get_item([\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"], -3)</code> returns <code>'a'</code>, and <code>get_item([\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"], 3)</code> returns <code>None</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def get_item(L, i):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(get_item([\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"], 1))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
