  {
   "xml_index": 11,
   "xml_name": "L07_011_Item In A Table",
   "display_name": "Item In A Table",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>A list can contain other lists. A list of lists is a natural way to store a <b>table</b>: each inner list is one <b>row</b>.</p>\n<pre>table = [[1, 2, 3],\n         [4, 5, 6],\n         [7, 8, 9]]</pre>\n<table style=\"border-collapse:separate;border-spacing:2px 0;margin:6px 0 4px\"><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px\"></td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">-3</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">-2</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">-1</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace\">table =</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(31,95,153);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">[1, 2, 3]</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(52,129,188);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">[4, 5, 6]</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(74,163,223);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">[7, 8, 9]</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px\"></td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">0</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">1</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">2</td></tr></table>\n<table style=\"border-collapse:separate;border-spacing:2px 0;margin:6px 0 4px\"><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px\"></td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">-3</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">-2</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">-1</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace\">table[1] =</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:#e2e6ea;color:#8a939c;border-radius:2px\">4</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:#e2e6ea;color:#8a939c;border-radius:2px\">5</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(74,163,223);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">6</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px\"></td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">0</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">1</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">2</td></tr></table><div style=\"font-size:13px;color:#8a939c;margin:0 0 10px\">table[1] is the row [4, 5, 6], and table[1][2] is 6</div>\n<p><code>table[1]</code> is the second row (itself a list), and <code>table[1][2]</code> takes item <code>2</code> of that row. So <code>table[r][c]</code> is the item in row <code>r</code>, column <code>c</code> (both counted from 0).</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>get_cell(table, r, c)</code>, which returns the item in row <code>r</code> and column <code>c</code> of the list of lists <code>table</code>. The indexes are always valid.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>get_cell([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6], [7, 8, 9]], 1, 2)</code> returns <code>6</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def get_cell(table, r, c):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(get_cell([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6], [7, 8, 9]], 1, 2))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
