  {
   "xml_index": 12,
   "xml_name": "L07_012_Row And Column Sums",
   "display_name": "Row And Column Sums",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>To add up a <b>row</b>, loop over the items of that row. To add up a <b>column</b>, loop over the <b>rows</b> and take the item at the same column from each one:</p>\n<pre>grid = [[2, 4], [6, 8], [10, 12]]\ntotal = 0\nfor row in grid:\n    total = total + row[1]      # the item in column 1 of this row\nprint(total)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>24</pre>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, loop or function must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write two functions for a table (a non-empty list of rows, all of the same non-zero length) of integers:</p>\n<ul><li><code>row_sum(table, r)</code> returns the sum of the items in row <code>r</code>;</li><li><code>col_sum(table, c)</code> returns the sum of the items in column <code>c</code>.</li></ul>\n<p>For example, <code>row_sum([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]], 1)</code> returns <code>15</code> and <code>col_sum([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]], 0)</code> returns <code>5</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def row_sum(table, r):\n    # write your code here\n\n\ndef col_sum(table, c):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(row_sum([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]], 1))\n# print(col_sum([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]], 0))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(row_sum([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]], 1))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "15",
