  {
   "xml_index": 19,
   "xml_name": "L07_019_Slicing Strings",
   "display_name": "Slicing Strings",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Everything you learned about indexing and slicing also works on <b>strings</b>. A string is a sequence of characters, and a slice of a string is a new, shorter string:</p>\n<table style=\"border-collapse:separate;border-spacing:2px 0;margin:6px 0 4px\"><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px\"></td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">-6</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">-5</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">-4</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">-3</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">-2</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">-1</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace\">s =</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(31,95,153);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">P</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(40,109,167);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">Y</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(48,122,181);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">T</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(57,136,195);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">H</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(65,149,209);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">O</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(74,163,223);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">N</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px\"></td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">0</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">1</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">2</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">3</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">4</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">5</td></tr></table>\n<pre>s = &quot;PYTHON&quot;\nprint(s[0], s[-1])\nprint(s[1:4])\nprint(s[:2], s[2:])\nprint(s[::-1])</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>P N\nYTH\nPY THON\nNOHTYP</pre>\n<p>A word is a <b>palindrome</b> if it reads the same backwards, like <code>\"madam\"</code> or <code>\"noon\"</code>: the string equals its own reverse.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write two functions, for a string <code>word</code> of lowercase letters:</p>\n<ul><li><code>inner(word)</code> returns <code>word</code> without its first and last characters (for a word of 2 letters or fewer, this is the empty string <code>\"\"</code>);</li><li><code>is_palindrome(word)</code> returns <code>True</code> if <code>word</code> reads the same backwards, otherwise <code>False</code>. The empty string counts as a palindrome.</li></ul>\n<p>For example, <code>inner(\"python\")</code> returns <code>\"ytho\"</code> and <code>is_palindrome(\"madam\")</code> returns <code>True</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def inner(word):\n    # write your code here\n\n\ndef is_palindrome(word):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(inner(\"python\"))\n# print(is_palindrome(\"madam\"))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
