  {
   "xml_index": 20,
   "xml_name": "L07_020_Slices Never Go Out Of Range",
   "display_name": "Slices Never Go Out Of Range",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Indexing past the end is an error, but <b>slicing is forgiving</b>: if <code>start</code> or <code>stop</code> goes past the end of the list, Python simply stops at the end. No <code>IndexError</code>:</p>\n<pre>L = [10, 20, 30]\nprint(L[1:100])\nprint(L[5:9])\nprint(L[:2])\nx = []\nprint(x[:2])</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>[20, 30]\n[]\n[10, 20]\n[]</pre>\n<p>This makes slices great for \"the first few items\" when the list might be shorter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>top_three(scores)</code>, which returns the first three items of the list <code>scores</code>, or all of them if there are fewer than three. Do not use <code>if</code>: a slice is enough.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>top_three([91, 85, 78, 66])</code> returns <code>[91, 85, 78]</code> and <code>top_three([50])</code> returns <code>[50]</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def top_three(scores):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(top_three([91, 85, 78, 66]))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
