  {
   "xml_index": 22,
   "xml_name": "L07_022_Change One Item",
   "display_name": "Change One Item",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Lists are <b>mutable</b>: they can be changed after they are made. Assigning to an index replaces that item:</p>\n<pre>L[i] = new_value</pre>\n<table style=\"border-collapse:separate;border-spacing:2px 0;margin:6px 0 4px\"><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace\">before:</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(31,95,153);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">&#x27;red&#x27;</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(52,129,188);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">&#x27;green&#x27;</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(74,163,223);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">&#x27;blue&#x27;</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px\"></td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">0</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">1</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">2</td></tr></table>\n<table style=\"border-collapse:separate;border-spacing:2px 0;margin:6px 0 4px\"><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace\">L[1] = &#x27;violet&#x27;:</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:#e2e6ea;color:#8a939c;border-radius:2px\">&#x27;red&#x27;</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(52,129,188);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">&#x27;violet&#x27;</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:#e2e6ea;color:#8a939c;border-radius:2px\">&#x27;blue&#x27;</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px\"></td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">0</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">1</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">2</td></tr></table>\n<p>This changes the list <b>itself</b>; no new list is made. So a function can change a list it is given, and the caller sees the change. Such a function does not need to return anything (it returns <code>None</code>); the tests call it, then print the list:</p>\n<pre>def paint_first(colours, c):\n    colours[0] = c\n\nmy_list = [&quot;red&quot;, &quot;green&quot;, &quot;blue&quot;]\npaint_first(my_list, &quot;white&quot;)\nprint(my_list)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>[&#x27;white&#x27;, &#x27;green&#x27;, &#x27;blue&#x27;]</pre>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>set_last(L, x)</code>, which replaces the <b>last</b> item of the non-empty list <code>L</code> with <code>x</code>. It changes <code>L</code> and returns nothing.</p>\n<p>For example, after <code>L = [1, 2, 3]</code> and <code>set_last(L, 9)</code>, <code>L</code> is <code>[1, 2, 9]</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def set_last(L, x):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# L = [1, 2, 3]; set_last(L, 9); print(L)\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "L = [1, 2, 3]\nset_last(L, 9)\nprint(L)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[1, 2, 9]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
