  {
   "xml_index": 23,
   "xml_name": "L07_023_Double Every Item In Place",
   "display_name": "Double Every Item In Place",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>To change <b>every</b> item of a list, loop over the <b>indexes</b> and assign to <code>L[i]</code>:</p>\n<pre>prices = [100, 250, 40]\nfor i in range(len(prices)):\n    prices[i] = prices[i] + 10\nprint(prices)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>[110, 260, 50]</pre>\n<p>A common mistake is to loop over the <b>items</b> instead. The loop variable only holds a copy of each value; changing it does <b>not</b> change the list:</p>\n<pre>prices = [100, 250, 40]\nfor p in prices:\n    p = p + 10       # changes only p, not the list\nprint(prices)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>[100, 250, 40]</pre>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, loop or function must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>double_all(L)</code>, which doubles every item of the list of integers <code>L</code>, <b>changing <code>L</code> itself</b>. It returns nothing. An empty list stays empty.</p>\n<p>For example, after <code>L = [1, 5, -2]</code> and <code>double_all(L)</code>, <code>L</code> is <code>[2, 10, -4]</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def double_all(L):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# L = [1, 5, -2]; double_all(L); print(L)\n",
