  {
   "xml_index": 24,
   "xml_name": "L07_024_Strings Cannot Change",
   "display_name": "Strings Cannot Change",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Lists are mutable, but <b>strings are not</b>: they are <b>immutable</b>. You can read <code>s[0]</code>, but you cannot assign to it:</p>\n<pre>s = &quot;cat&quot;\ns[0] = &quot;b&quot;</pre>\n<p>gives:</p>\n<pre style=\"color:#b3322a\">TypeError: &#x27;str&#x27; object does not support item assignment</pre>\n<p>To \"change\" a string, build a <b>new</b> string from pieces, for example with slices and <code>+</code>:</p>\n<pre>s = &quot;cat&quot;\nt = &quot;b&quot; + s[1:]\nprint(t)\nprint(s)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>bat\ncat</pre>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>replace_at(word, i, ch)</code>, which returns a new string: <code>word</code> with the character at index <code>i</code> replaced by the one-character string <code>ch</code>. The index <code>i</code> is from 0 to <code>len(word) - 1</code>.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>replace_at(\"cat\", 0, \"b\")</code> returns <code>\"bat\"</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def replace_at(word, i, ch):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(replace_at(\"cat\", 0, \"b\"))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
