  {
   "xml_index": 25,
   "xml_name": "L07_025_Two Names One List",
   "display_name": "Two Names One List",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Assigning a list to another name does <b>not</b> copy it. After <code>b = a</code>, both names refer to the <b>same</b> list, like two labels on one box. This is called <b>aliasing</b>. A change made through either name is seen through both:</p>\n<table style=\"border-collapse:collapse;margin:6px 0 10px\"><tr><td style=\"font-family:monospace;padding:2px 8px\">a</td><td style=\"padding:0 6px\">&rarr;</td><td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"vertical-align:middle\"><table style=\"border-collapse:separate;border-spacing:2px 0;margin:6px 0 4px\"><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace\"></td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(31,95,153);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">1</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(52,129,188);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">2</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(74,163,223);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">3</td></tr></table></td></tr><tr><td style=\"font-family:monospace;padding:2px 8px\">b</td><td style=\"padding:0 6px\">&rarr;</td></tr></table><div style=\"font-size:13px;color:#8a939c;margin:0 0 10px\">after b = a: two names, ONE list</div>\n<pre>a = [1, 2, 3]\nb = a\nb.append(4)\nprint(a)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>[1, 2, 3, 4]</pre>\n<p>To get an independent list, make a <b>copy</b>: <code>L.copy()</code> or the slice <code>L[:]</code>. Joining with <code>+</code> also makes a new list.</p>\n<p>The code below should return a <b>new</b> list with <code>x</code> added at the end, and leave the original list unchanged. It has an aliasing bug: the original list gets changed too. Fix it.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> fix the function <code>with_item(L, x)</code> so that it returns a new list (the items of <code>L</code> followed by <code>x</code>) and does <b>not</b> change <code>L</code>.</p>\n<p>For example, after <code>L = [1, 2]</code>, <code>with_item(L, 3)</code> returns <code>[1, 2, 3]</code> and <code>L</code> is still <code>[1, 2]</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def with_item(L, x):\n    new = L\n    new.append(x)\n    return new\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# L = [1, 2]; print(with_item(L, 3)); print(L)\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "L = [1, 2]\nprint(with_item(L, 3))\nprint(L)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[1, 2, 3]\n[1, 2]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "L = []\nprint(with_item(L, 'a'))\nprint(L)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "['a']\n[]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "L = list(map(int, input().split()))\nM = with_item(L, 0)\nprint(M)\nprint(L)",
     "stdin": "5 6 7",
     "expected": "[5, 6, 7, 0]\n[5, 6, 7]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "L = list(map(int, input().split()))\nM = with_item(L, 9)\nprint(M, L)",
     "stdin": "1",
     "expected": "[1, 9] [1]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 26,
   "xml_name": "L07_026_A Function That Changes Its Argument",
   "display_name": "A Function That Changes Its Argument",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>When you pass a list to a function, the parameter becomes <b>another name for the same list</b> (aliasing again). So if the function changes the list, the caller's list changes too.</p>\n<p>Such a function usually returns nothing, so calling <code>print(f(L))</code> prints <code>None</code>. What we want to see is the list:</p>\n<pre>def add_star(names):\n    for i in range(len(names)):\n        names[i] = names[i] + &quot;*&quot;\n\nteam = [&quot;Asha&quot;, &quot;Ravi&quot;]\nresult = add_star(team)\nprint(team)\nprint(result)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>[&#x27;Asha*&#x27;, &#x27;Ravi*&#x27;]\nNone</pre>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, loop or function must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>add_bonus(marks, bonus)</code>, which adds <code>bonus</code> to every mark in the list of integers <code>marks</code>, but never lets a mark go above 100 (a mark that would go above 100 becomes 100). It changes <code>marks</code> and returns nothing.</p>\n<p>For example, after <code>m = [50, 95, 70]</code> and <code>add_bonus(m, 10)</code>, <code>m</code> is <code>[60, 100, 80]</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# Define add_bonus(marks, bonus): change the list marks itself; return nothing.\n\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# m = [50, 95, 70]; add_bonus(m, 10); print(m)\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "m = [50, 95, 70]\nr = add_bonus(m, 10)\nprint(m)\nprint(r)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[60, 100, 80]\nNone",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
