  {
   "xml_index": 27,
   "xml_name": "L07_027_Make A Changed Copy",
   "display_name": "Make A Changed Copy",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Sometimes you want a changed version of a list <b>and</b> want to keep the original. Then:</p>\n<ol><li>make a copy with <code>L.copy()</code> or <code>L[:]</code>;</li><li>change the copy;</li><li>return the copy.</li></ol>\n<pre>original = [3, 6, 9]\ncopy = original[:]\ncopy[0] = 100\nprint(original)\nprint(copy)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>[3, 6, 9]\n[100, 6, 9]</pre>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>with_changed(L, i, x)</code>, which returns a new list equal to <code>L</code> but with the item at index <code>i</code> replaced by <code>x</code>. The list <code>L</code> must not change. <code>i</code> is a valid index (positive or negative).</p>\n<p>For example, after <code>L = [3, 6, 9]</code>, <code>with_changed(L, 1, 0)</code> returns <code>[3, 0, 9]</code> and <code>L</code> is still <code>[3, 6, 9]</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# Define with_changed(L, i, x): return a changed COPY; do not change L.\n\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# L = [3, 6, 9]; print(with_changed(L, 1, 0)); print(L)\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
