  {
   "xml_index": 29,
   "xml_name": "L07_029_Append Versus Extend",
   "display_name": "Append Versus Extend",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Both <code>append</code> and <code>extend</code> add to the end of a list, but they are different when the thing you add is a list:</p>\n<ul><li><code>L.append(x)</code> adds <b>one</b> item, <code>x</code>. If <code>x</code> is a list, it goes in as a single (nested) item.</li><li><code>L.extend(M)</code> adds <b>each item</b> of <code>M</code>, one by one.</li></ul>\n<pre>a = [1, 2]\na.append([3, 4])\nprint(a, len(a))\nb = [1, 2]\nb.extend([3, 4])\nprint(b, len(b))</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>[1, 2, [3, 4]] 3\n[1, 2, 3, 4] 4</pre>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>add_all(L, items)</code>, which adds every item of the list <code>items</code> to the end of the list <code>L</code>, in order. It changes <code>L</code> and returns nothing.</p>\n<p>For example, after <code>L = [1, 2]</code> and <code>add_all(L, [3, 4])</code>, <code>L</code> is <code>[1, 2, 3, 4]</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# Define add_all(L, items): add each item of items to the end of L.\n\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# L = [1, 2]; add_all(L, [3, 4]); print(L)\n",
