   "xml_index": 30,
   "xml_name": "L07_030_Joining Lists With Plus",
   "display_name": "Joining Lists With Plus",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>The <code>+</code> operator joins two lists into a <b>new</b> list; neither original list changes. <code>L += M</code> adds the items of <code>M</code> to <code>L</code> itself (like <code>extend</code>).</p>\n<pre>front = [1, 2]\nback = [8, 9]\nboth = front + back\nprint(both)\nprint(front, back)\nfront += [3]\nprint(front)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>[1, 2, 8, 9]\n[1, 2] [8, 9]\n[1, 2, 3]</pre>\n<p>Remember: <code>+</code> only joins a list with another <b>list</b>. To add a single value <code>x</code>, write <code>L + [x]</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>sandwich(L, x)</code>, which returns a new list: <code>x</code>, then all the items of <code>L</code>, then <code>x</code> again. <code>L</code> must not change.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>sandwich([5, 6], 0)</code> returns <code>[0, 5, 6, 0]</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# Define sandwich(L, x): return [x] + L + [x] as a new list.\n\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(sandwich([5, 6], 0))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(sandwich([5, 6], 0))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[0, 5, 6, 0]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "L = []\nprint(sandwich(L, 'b'))\nprint(L)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "['b', 'b']\n[]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "L = list(map(int, input().split()))\nprint(sandwich(L, 1))\nprint(L)",
     "stdin": "7 8 9",
     "expected": "[1, 7, 8, 9, 1]\n[7, 8, 9]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "L = list(map(int, input().split()))\nprint(sandwich(L, -1))",
     "stdin": "0",
     "expected": "[-1, 0, -1]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 31,
   "xml_name": "L07_031_Repeating With Star",
   "display_name": "Repeating With Star",
   "questiontext_html": "<p><code>L * n</code> makes a new list with the items of <code>L</code> repeated <code>n</code> times. It is a quick way to make a list of a given length filled with the same value:</p>\n<pre>print([0] * 5)\nprint([&quot;ha&quot;] * 3)\nprint([1, 2] * 3)\nprint([7] * 0)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>[0, 0, 0, 0, 0]\n[&#x27;ha&#x27;, &#x27;ha&#x27;, &#x27;ha&#x27;]\n[1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2]\n[]</pre>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>pattern(items, n, fill)</code>, which returns a new list: the items of <code>items</code> repeated <code>n</code> times, followed by the value <code>fill</code> repeated <code>n</code> times. <code>n</code> is a non-negative integer (int &gt;= 0).</p>\n<p>For example, <code>pattern([1, 2], 2, 0)</code> returns <code>[1, 2, 1, 2, 0, 0]</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# Define pattern(items, n, fill).\n\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(pattern([1, 2], 2, 0))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(pattern([1, 2], 2, 0))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[1, 2, 1, 2, 0, 0]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(pattern(['a'], 3, 'b'))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "['a', 'a', 'a', 'b', 'b', 'b']",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(pattern([5, 6], 0, 9))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(pattern([7, 8, 9], 1, -1))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[7, 8, 9, -1]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(pattern([], 4, 1))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[1, 1, 1, 1]",
     "extra": "",
