  {
   "xml_index": 36,
   "xml_name": "L07_036_Remove Every Match",
   "display_name": "Remove Every Match",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Since <code>remove</code> deletes only the first match, removing <b>all</b> matches needs a loop. A simple way: keep removing while the value is still in the list.</p>\n<pre>while x in L:\n    L.remove(x)</pre>\n<p>(Another way is to build a <b>new</b> list of the items you want to keep. Both are fine; here the list itself must change.)</p>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, loop or function must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>remove_all(L, x)</code>, which removes every item equal to <code>x</code> from the list <code>L</code>. It changes <code>L</code> and returns nothing.</p>\n<p>For example, after <code>L = [1, 2, 1, 3, 1]</code> and <code>remove_all(L, 1)</code>, <code>L</code> is <code>[2, 3]</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# Define remove_all(L, x): remove every x from L.\n\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# L = [1, 2, 1, 3, 1]; remove_all(L, 1); print(L)\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "L = [1, 2, 1, 3, 1]\nremove_all(L, 1)\nprint(L)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[2, 3]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
