     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 39,
   "xml_name": "L07_039_Sort Versus Sorted",
   "display_name": "Sort Versus Sorted",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>There are two ways to sort, and the difference is mutability again:</p>\n<ul><li><code>L.sort()</code> sorts <b>the list itself</b> and returns <code>None</code>;</li><li><code>sorted(L)</code> returns a <b>new</b> sorted list and leaves <code>L</code> unchanged.</li></ul>\n<p>Both sort from small to large; add <code>reverse=True</code> for large to small.</p>\n<pre>L = [3, 1, 2]\nS = sorted(L)\nprint(S, L)\nr = L.sort()\nprint(L, r)\nprint(sorted([3, 1, 2], reverse=True))</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>[1, 2, 3] [3, 1, 2]\n[1, 2, 3] None\n[3, 2, 1]</pre>\n<p>A common mistake is <code>L = L.sort()</code>, which sets <code>L</code> to <code>None</code>!</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>smallest_three(L)</code>, which returns a new list with the three smallest items of the list of integers <code>L</code> (it has at least three items), from small to large. <code>L</code> must <b>not</b> change.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>smallest_three([7, 2, 9, 4, 1])</code> returns <code>[1, 2, 4]</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# Define smallest_three(L): do not change L.\n\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# L = [7, 2, 9, 4, 1]; print(smallest_three(L)); print(L)\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "L = [7, 2, 9, 4, 1]\nprint(smallest_three(L))\nprint(L)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[1, 2, 4]\n[7, 2, 9, 4, 1]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(smallest_three([5, 5, 5]))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[5, 5, 5]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "L = list(map(int, input().split()))\nprint(smallest_three(L))\nprint(L)",
     "stdin": "10 -3 8 -3 0 2",
     "expected": "[-3, -3, 0]\n[10, -3, 8, -3, 0, 2]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "L = list(map(int, input().split()))\nprint(smallest_three(L))",
     "stdin": "3 2 1",
     "expected": "[1, 2, 3]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 40,
   "xml_name": "L07_040_Reverse In Place",
   "display_name": "Reverse In Place",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Like sorting, reversing comes in two forms:</p>\n<ul><li><code>L.reverse()</code> reverses <b>the list itself</b> (returns <code>None</code>);</li><li><code>L[::-1]</code> gives a <b>new</b> reversed list.</li></ul>\n<pre>L = [1, 2, 3]\nR = L[::-1]\nprint(L, R)\nL.reverse()\nprint(L)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>[1, 2, 3] [3, 2, 1]\n[3, 2, 1]</pre>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>reverse_ends(L, k)</code>, which reverses <b>only the first <code>k</code> items</b> of the list <code>L</code>, in place (the rest stays as it is). It changes <code>L</code> and returns nothing. <code>k</code> is an integer from 0 to <code>len(L)</code>.</p>\n<p>Hint: a slice can also be <b>assigned to</b>: <code>L[:k] = something</code> replaces the first <code>k</code> items.</p>\n<p>For example, after <code>L = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]</code> and <code>reverse_ends(L, 3)</code>, <code>L</code> is <code>[3, 2, 1, 4, 5]</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# Define reverse_ends(L, k): reverse the first k items of L in place.\n\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# L = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]; reverse_ends(L, 3); print(L)\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "L = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\nreverse_ends(L, 3)\nprint(L)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[3, 2, 1, 4, 5]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "L = [1, 2, 3]\nreverse_ends(L, 0)\nprint(L)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[1, 2, 3]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "L = [1, 2, 3]\nreverse_ends(L, 3)\nprint(L)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[3, 2, 1]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
